/*!
    \page building-for-harmonyos.html
    \title Building \QC for HarmonyOS

    \QC runs on HarmonyOS devices, such as tablets, as an application package.
    On the device, it can build applications and run and debug them. The
    scripts in \c scripts/harmonyos in the \QC sources build two packages:

    \list
        \li \c qtcreator.hap, which holds \QC, its plugins, and the tools
            it executes on the device. Those come in a native package,
            \c qtctools.hnp, because the device executes nothing that an
            application writes itself.
        \li \c qtcrunner.hap, which runs what \QC builds on the device.
            From its sandbox, \QC cannot install or start an application
            package. Instead, it hands the application over to this
            package, which loads it.
    \endlist

    The build runs in a Docker image that the scripts create locally. It holds
    only the host tools, so the result does not depend on the machine that it
    runs on.

    \section1 Requirements

    The build has been tested on Linux on x86_64. It needs:

    \list
        \li Docker and Python 3.
        \li Network access. The scripts download the Ubuntu base image, the
            Ubuntu packages, and the sources of the libraries and tools that
            they build. The sources are pinned to fixed versions. If a
            download from a mirror fails, for example with a certificate
            error, the build tries the next source on its own. Such errors
            in the output only matter if the build stops at them.
        \li Qt for HarmonyOS from the Qt Online Installer, with the desktop Qt
            of the same version installed beside it. For example,
            \c ~/Qt/6.12.0/harmonyos_arm64_v8a and \c ~/Qt/6.12.0/gcc_64.
            The tools that the build runs on the host must come from the same
            Qt sources as the libraries, so the scripts check for the
            \c sha1s.txt file beside them.
        \li The HarmonyOS command-line tools of the SDK version that the
            application template of the Qt for HarmonyOS names. Qt 6.12
            requires 6.1.0(23), which the command-line tools 6.1.0.850
            carry. Download \uicontrol {Command Line Tools} for Linux from
            the \l {https://developer.huawei.com/consumer/en/download/}
            {HUAWEI Developer download page}. The scripts take the downloaded
            zip file as it is and unpack it into the output directory once.
        \li Optionally, the device-hosted native SDK, which is published as
            \c native-ohos-x64. Without it, the scripts download a pinned
            OpenHarmony SDK build that contains it.
        \li The material for signing the packages: a keystore, a file that
            holds the passphrase of the keystore and its key, and a profile
            and a certificate for each package. The two packages have
            different bundle names, so they need different profiles. Debug
            profiles from AppGallery Connect are enough, and they need no
            ACL permissions. Each profile must list the devices that the
            package is installed on. Without signing material, the
            scripts build the packages but leave them unsigned, and a
            device does not install unsigned packages.
    \endlist

    \section1 Building the Packages

    To build both packages, enter:

    \badcode
    scripts/harmonyos/build_creator_for_harmonyos.py \
        --qt ~/Qt/6.12.0/harmonyos_arm64_v8a \
        --sdk <command-line tools zip, or the directory it was unpacked into> \
        --path <output directory> \
        --keystore <keystore.p12> --key-alias <alias> \
        --keystore-password-file <file> \
        --profile <profile.p7b> --certificate <certificate.cer> \
        --runner-profile <profile.p7b> --runner-certificate <certificate.cer> \
        --runner-bundle <bundle name>
    \endcode

    The script runs the following stages in order. Each stage is a script in
    the same directory, which you can also run on its own:

    \table
    \header
        \li Stage
        \li Script
        \li Result in the output directory
    \row
        \li \c packages
        \li \c install_third_party.py
        \li The libraries that Qt needs at runtime, such as ICU and
            fontconfig, in \c packages/arm64-ohos.
    \row
        \li \c tools
        \li \c build_qt_tools.py
        \li The Qt tools that \QC runs on the device, in \c tools.
    \row
        \li \c creator
        \li \c build_creator.py
        \li The \QC build, in \c creator.
    \row
        \li \c toolchain
        \li \c build_device_toolchain.py
        \li The compiler, CMake, Ninja, and debugger that \QC builds and
            debugs with on the device, in \c toolchain. Without this stage,
            \QC runs on the device but cannot build there.
    \row
        \li \c hap
        \li \c build_hap.py
        \li \c qtcreator.hap, or \c qtcreator-unsigned.hap.
    \row
        \li \c runner
        \li \c build_runner_hap.py
        \li \c qtcrunner.hap, or \c qtcrunner-unsigned.hap.
    \endtable

    The output directory needs about 21 GB, 6.4 GB of which are the
    command-line tools when \c --sdk is the zip file.

    To run only some stages, list them in \c --stages. A later run reuses what
    an earlier one left in the output directory, so after the first run, a
    stage takes little time unless its inputs changed.

    \section1 Installing the Packages

    To install the packages on a device that \c hdc is connected to, enter:

    \badcode
    hdc install -r <output directory>/qtcreator.hap
    hdc install -r <output directory>/qtcrunner.hap
    \endcode

    For \c hdc to install them, the device must be in developer mode, and
    the profiles that the packages are signed with must list the device.

    \section1 Starting \QC on the Device

    To start \QC from \c hdc, enter:

    \badcode
    hdc shell aa start -a QAbility -b <bundle name>
    \endcode

    The bundle name is the one that you passed in \c --bundle, which is
    \c org.qtproject.qtcreator by default.

    \section1 Building, Running, and Debugging on the Device

    When \QC starts on the device, it adds the device itself as
    \uicontrol {HarmonyOS Build Device (This Device)} and creates a kit for it.
    The kit uses the compiler, CMake, and LLDB from the \c toolchain stage and
    the Qt that the package holds, so a package built without that stage has
    no kit to build with.

    When you run or debug an application, \QC hands it over to the runner, so
    \c qtcrunner.hap must be installed on the device as well. To stop the
    application, stop it in \QC as usual.

    \section1 Controlling \QC over MCP

    For development, \QC on the device can run its MCP server, so that an
    agent or a script on the host can drive it. To start the server on a
    port, pass \c --mcp-port to the build script. The package then starts
    \QC with the McpServer plugin loaded and its \c ui_* tools turned on.

    The server only accepts requests that carry its token. Pass a file that
    holds the token in \c --mcp-token-file, or let the script write a new
    token into \c qtcreator.mcp-token beside the package. Anyone on the device
    can read the arguments that the token is passed in, so do not use MCP
    packages outside development.

    To reach the server from the host, forward the port with \c hdc:

    \badcode
    hdc fport tcp:<port> tcp:<port>
    \endcode

    Then send the requests to \c http://127.0.0.1:<port>/ with the token in an
    \c {Authorization: Bearer} header.
*/
